course: dictionary
lesson: 9063
title: Small words with big meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Slight means small, not much, or not important. «She had a slight cold», so the cold was small and not serious. Slight can also describe a thin, small body. Ask yourself: is this thing small or not very much? If yes, slight probably fits.
explain: Slight has a second job too. A slight is a small insult, and to slight someone means to ignore them or treat them as unimportant. «Ignoring my hello felt like a slight.» The feeling here is hurt, not a small amount of anything.
explain: Look for phrases. «Not in the slightest» means not at all. When the Hatter says he has not the slightest idea, he means he has no idea at all. Slightest is the smallest form of slight, and it can mean zero.
explain: Some words sound quiet but hide a big meaning. «Slight problem in the kitchen, love!» shouted over a burst pipe is an understatement: it sounds small, but it needs help fast. Context and tone tell you the real size of the problem.
fact: Slight means small, not much, or not important.
fact: «Slightly» means a little bit, as in «slightly warm».
fact: «Not in the slightest» means not at all.
fact: To slight someone means to insult them by ignoring them or treating them as unimportant.
fact: An understatement, like «a slight problem», makes something sound smaller than it really is.
exercise: «She had a slight cold for one day.» What does slight mean here? | Very long and painful | Icy and wet | Loud and noisy | *Small, not serious
exercise: «Ignoring my hello felt like a slight.» What is a slight in this sentence? | *A small insult | A quiet song | A small present | A sunny morning
exercise: When the Hatter says he has not the slightest idea, what does he mean? | *He has no idea at all | He has a small idea | He knows the answer well | He is sure of it
test: In «a slight difference between the twins», what does slight mean? | A large and clear change | *A small amount | A cold and wet day | A sudden crash
test: Which phrase means "not at all"? | *not in the slightest | a slight chance | a slight cold | slighted by a friend
exam: Mum says, «Slight problem with the car, love», but smoke is coming from the bonnet. What does the context show? | The car is perfectly fine, so there is nothing to do | Mum is making a joke about a slight | Slight means the car is thin and small | *Her words hide a bigger problem, so it needs help now
